You are the worker for task file `tasks/$TEND_TASK_ID.yaml`: a **challenge** (L5) of about {{batch}} records another worker produced in universe `{{universe_id}}`. Your job is to refute them, not to confirm them.

Read the task file and its manifest `{{tasks_dir}}/$TEND_TASK_ID.json`: for each source it lists the records that are yours, each with its `target` (`location`, `absence`, `unresolved`, `scope`, `prominence` or `coverage`), its passage `item`, where to read it (`text_span` in `text.txt`, `section`, `quote`; `span` is a `layer.txt` offset), for a location its `location_key`, `library`, `declaration` and `relation` — with what the worker wrote of it (`claims`, `clauses`, `instantiation`, `step`, `convention`, `note`) and the passage's other locations (`passage_locations`, read-only: context, not records of yours) — and for a coverage claim its `parts` (each part's `location_key`, `library`, `declaration`, `relation` and `rank`) and the worker's `note`. Your task holds every required record of a passage. Read the passage yourself in `{{sources_dir}}/<source-id>/text.txt` before you judge anything.

## How to judge each record
- **First, write the claim list of each passage you judge**, in your own words, before any verdict on it: under the document's `claims`, `"<item>": [{"id": "c1", "text": "<one clause>"}, …]` — each conclusion, each direction of an iff, existence and uniqueness separately, each computation of an example; never proof narration (PD5), and a stated reason ("it holds because X") only when X is a declarative mathematical statement no neighbouring passage already states (PD9). Every verdict on a location, a coverage claim, an absence or an unresolved outcome rests on it: a refute, a downgrade and an uphold as much as a confirm. What the worker wrote (its `claims`, `clauses`, `instantiation`, `step`, `convention`, `note`) is a claim to check, never evidence: the worker may have listed too few clauses. Split a worker's clause if you must, but never drop one: before you keep `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` or `broadMatch`, your list has at least as many claims as the worker's.
- **A location** is refuted by default.
  - Check it against your claim list first (the challenger's rule, FM7 below), then read the declaration's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`), and either **confirm** it with a quoted fragment of the signature that states the passage, **downgrade** its relation to the one it really has, or **refute** it when it does not state the passage at all.
  - A verdict that leaves a relation that counts (a confirm of one, or a downgrade to one) names the ids of your claims the declaration states, in `clauses`; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` stand only when they name every claim — otherwise downgrade to `hasPart` — and a downgrade to `relatedMatch` names none.
  - A record whose own `note` says the declaration does not state a clause of the passage is, by its own account, a `hasPart`, unless you find the note itself wrong: check it.
  - Instantiate the declaration at the passage's objects: check the record's `instantiation` against the signature, and when it has none and the signature takes an instance argument, write your own (`instantiation`) before you keep a relation that counts. If the declaration's setting neither contains nor is contained in the passage's — no instance exists at the pin for the passage's objects, or the declaration needs finite dimension or finite rank and the objects the passage is about have neither, so no instance of the passage is one of the declaration's — downgrade it to `relatedMatch`. If its setting is a special case of the passage's (a field where the passage has a commutative ring, ℕ where it has ℤ), it is `narrowMatch` (R3), never `relatedMatch`. A `broadMatch` by instantiation stands only when what is left after instantiating closes by decidable arithmetic (`omega`, `norm_num`, `decide`); a bridge through a named lemma makes it `relatedMatch` (PD8).
  - A `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` stands only when the declaration alone entails what it is credited with: a lemma the proof merely uses, one that needs a second declaration to reach the clause, or a neighbouring passage's fact is `relatedMatch`. A verdict that leaves a `hasPart` or `narrowMatch` says what the declaration alone entails of this passage in `step` — on a proof the displayed step, on any other passage the clause — a downgrade's own, and a confirm's when the record names none.
  - A declaration also defined where the passage is not (Lean's x/0 = 0) is judged by R1's three-way test (PD7): an `exactMatch` when it agrees on the passage's domain and every case outside it is trivial, a `broadMatch` when some case outside it has real mathematical content, a `closeMatch` only for a difference where both sides are defined. A record whose `convention` is `junk_value` was judged before PD7: when every case outside the domain is trivial it is the `exactMatch` a challenge cannot raise it to, so confirm it as it stands and say so in the witness; when one has real content, downgrade it to `broadMatch`.
  - An overturn location carries its own `clauses`, `instantiation` and `step`, as a worker's does.
- **An absence or an unresolved outcome** is hunted: search first, harder and differently than the worker did (`{{search_command}} <query words…>` with other names, the objects the statement is about, the generalisation a library would state), with a query of its own for every clause of your claim list — the worker's receipt (`query_clauses`) shows which clauses it searched, and a false absence is usually a clause nobody searched — and, for a worked example, one for the general theorem it instantiates{{#if checkouts_dir}}, and grep the pinned library checkouts under `{{checkouts_dir}}`{{/if}}. Check each candidate against each claim: one that states a clause alone is a `hasPart` location. **Overturn** it with the locations you found, or **uphold** it, listing the queries you ran (`queries`, at least {{min_queries}} distinct) tagged with your claims (`query_clauses`, every query tagged, each claim with a query of its own).
- **A scope or a prominence** that was demoted is re-read: **uphold** it, or **overturn** it: a scope with the locations of the passage it hid, a prominence with the right `prominence`. A `subject` prominence is refuted by default, since every record of a subject passage carries the article's own Wikidata id: **uphold** it only when the passage states what the article as a whole is about, and otherwise **overturn** it to `central` (a named result or definition the article states) or `supporting`.
- **A coverage claim** says that the `hasPart` locations in its `parts` together state every clause of the passage. A confirmed claim raises the passage's derived status (to formalized when every part is proved or data, to statement_only otherwise) and confirms with it only the parts whose own record the plan did not require (none, once the passage's prominence is overturned to `subject` or `central`: those parts are then owed a challenge of their own); give a verdict for every part record the task lists as required, judged on its own. So a claim is refuted by default, and more strictly than a location: **refute** it unless every clause of your claim list is stated by a part AND no part adds a hypothesis the passage does not have (a stronger assumption, an extra instance argument, a special case, another object). Read each part's signature (`{{show_command}} <library> <declaration>`), and **confirm** only when you can name the clauses each part states: `part_clauses`, `{"<part's location_key>": ["c1"], …}`, one entry per part, together naming every claim; otherwise refute, naming the clause no part states or the hypothesis a part adds. **Refute a claim one of whose parts alone states every clause of your claim list** (PD6): that part is one location with its own relation, not a part, and the claim stands on no such part — judge the part's own record on its own. The worker's `note` is a claim to check, not evidence. A refute leaves the parts as they are: judge each part's own location record on its own, and never confirm a claim while you refute or weaken one of its parts.

**The challenger's rule.** Before any downgrade or refute:

- **FM7** — before you downgrade or refute, check the record against your claim list of the passage. Apply D1: an exact or close match to an equivalence or other data that states the theorem formalizes it, so a bijection is the data form of a cardinality equation and stays `closeMatch`. Keep definitionally linked declarations consistent: two declarations on one passage defined one from the other, or one object in two encodings, get the same relation unless their statements differ — your task holds every required location of a passage, and each location record shows the passage's other locations (`passage_locations`), read-only. A trivial special case is `narrowMatch` (PD3), never a refute.

## How to judge a relation: rules R1–R7
Judge each record pairwise, the passage against the one declaration, by these rules:

- **R1** — `closeMatch` only for a convention: notation, encoding, normalisation, renaming, unfolding, bundling (the location's `convention`). A strengthening, a generalisation or a weakening is never one, and neither is a junk value (PD7). Where the declaration's map f′ : X′ → Y extends the passage's f : X → Y (X ⊂ X′): if f′ agrees with f on X and every case outside X is mathematically trivial — a junk or default value (x/0 = 0, `riemannZeta` at its pole) or an extra case that reduces to a tautology (p = q, where the statement becomes IsSquare 0 ↔ IsSquare 0) — it is `exactMatch`; if it agrees on X and some case outside X has real mathematical content, it is `broadMatch` (R2), a generalisation; if f′(x) ≠ f(x) at some x where both are defined, it is `closeMatch` (a convention) or weaker.
- **R2** — `broadMatch` when the passage follows from the declaration by instantiation or weakening (a general codomain, a strict derivative for a plain one, an iff for an if, all primes for a finite table), with no hypothesis stronger than the passage's; a stronger hypothesis makes it at most `narrowMatch` (R3).
- **R3** — `narrowMatch` when the declaration is the passage at special objects: the passage, instantiated at the declaration's objects, says what the declaration says (a natural-number statement for an integer one, a field for a commutative ring, the n = 1 case). It is also `narrowMatch` when the declaration proves the passage's conclusion, or a stronger one, under stronger hypotheses that some of the passage's own cases meet (global continuity where the passage has local): a special case (PD4).
- **R4** — `hasPart` when the declaration states one clause of a passage that says several things; `exactMatch`, `closeMatch` and `broadMatch` only when it states every clause.
- **R5** — `relatedMatch` when the connection needs another theorem, or the two statements are incomparable: a setting that neither contains nor is contained in the passage's (a finite-dimensional lemma where the passage's spaces are infinite-dimensional), or a declaration with stronger hypotheses whose conclusion does not give the passage's (one whose conclusion does is R3's special case). A declaration that follows from the passage by weakening states part of it at most: `hasPart` when what it states is one of the passage's clauses, else `relatedMatch`. So `hasPart` needs the declaration alone, instantiated at the passage's objects, to entail a clause the passage asserts, and `narrowMatch` needs the passage, instantiated at the declaration's objects, to say what the declaration says.
- **R6** — relations are pairwise: what else the universe holds never changes a record's relation.
- **R7** — a definition passage is judged on the data it defines; bundled proof fields are packaging (R1's bundling).

The policy defaults, the maintainer's (each overridable, never by you):

- **PD1** — a definition stated over a general type parameter where the passage has a specific one (any ring where the passage has ℂ) is `broadMatch` (R2).
- **PD2** — a statement over ℕ where the passage has ℤ is `narrowMatch` (R3).
- **PD3** — a trivial or degenerate special case (the n = 1 case of a matrix identity) is `narrowMatch`, never vacuous: it is not refuted for being trivial.
- **PD4** — a declaration that proves the passage's conclusion, or a stronger one, under stronger hypotheses that some of the passage's own cases meet (global instead of local continuity) is `narrowMatch` (R3), not `relatedMatch` and not `broadMatch`.
- **PD5** — only definitions, theorems, equations and declarative statements about mathematical objects are passages. Proof narration and commentary (what a proof uses, how it goes) is no passage: it is `expository`, never located, and has no status. Inside a proof, each definition, theorem, equation or declarative statement is a passage of its own. Linking a single word to the Lean definition it names is a separate feature, not a passage.
- **PD6** — a part of a coverage claim that alone states every clause is no part: it is one location with its own relation (a centralizer defined in any ring, where the passage has a matrix's centralizer, is `broadMatch`, PD1), and a coverage claim stands only on parts that each state some of the clauses.
- **PD7** — whether a case outside the passage's domain is trivial (R1) is a judgment: an `exactMatch` or `broadMatch` that rests on it says why in its `note`, and a case with real mathematical content makes a generalisation, never a junk value. (PD3's trivial case is the other way round: a declaration that states only a degenerate case of the passage.)
- **PD8** — a passage follows from a declaration by instantiation (R2) only when, after instantiating, what is left closes by decidable arithmetic (`omega`, `norm_num`, `decide`); a bridge that needs a named lemma (`Nat.mul_mod`, a parity lemma for (−1)^n) makes it `relatedMatch` (R5).
- **PD9** — a stated reason ("it holds because X") makes X a claim only when X is a declarative mathematical statement that no neighbouring passage already states.

